00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:27.340
Hart Montgomery: So, as you all know, you know, today and in the past.

107
00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:43.440
Hart Montgomery: LF Decentralized Trust and formerly Hyperledger, have been a collection of decentralized technologies, right? Heavily focused on, sort of, blockchain, digital identity, interoperability. But recently, we've seen

108
00:18:43.850 --> 00:18:55.000
Hart Montgomery: We've seen maintainers and contributors branch out into things like privacy with Paladin, cryptographic tools and key management, like Loch Ness.

109
00:18:57.020 --> 00:19:11.810
Hart Montgomery: and ZK tools and things like Minakawa. So, we just wanted to sort of, take a look and talk about, you know, oops, I'm sorry, sort of where we think, things are going.

110
00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:25.319
Hart Montgomery: You know, as we've seen in 2025, we've really seen the growth of decentralized identity. We've seen more open-source smart contracts and cryptographic tools and protocols.

111
00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:34.949
Hart Montgomery: I think 2026 is gonna be a big year where we start to see a lot of open source standardized smart contracts, and I'll go into that more later.

112
00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:48.239
Hart Montgomery: But as we continue, right, as things move forward, we're expecting to see a lot more digital public infrastructure. Are people here familiar with EBSI?

113
00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:55.069
Hart Montgomery: Yes, no, baby? Feel free to speak up.

114
00:19:57.940 --> 00:20:04.379
Giuseppe Bertone: Yeah, I worked with that in the past. I'm from Italy, so yeah, that's why.

115
00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:05.900
Hart Montgomery: Great. Great.

116
00:20:06.870 --> 00:20:22.219
Hart Montgomery: So, you know, we have things like EBSI, LakChain, Elastria, so we're really starting to see growth in this area, not to mention things like the EUDI wallet infrastructure, and lots of different U.S. states doing digital identity as well.

117
00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:40.790
Hart Montgomery: We're seeing big growth in privacy-preserving computation. MPC is becoming very, very… it's growing a lot, if you're familiar with that technology. And we're seeing a big push towards tokenization and digital asset infrastructure, so token standards…

118
00:20:40.790 --> 00:20:51.699
Hart Montgomery: Things like that. I don't know if you all listened to the open asset discussion, but it's likely we'll see token standards and LFET very soon.

119
00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:58.799
Hart Montgomery: And then finally, you know, looking forward in the sort of longer-term future,

120
00:20:59.020 --> 00:21:17.310
Hart Montgomery: we believe that a lot of the way that ML is going to have to work is in a decentralized way, right? You know, if Visa and MasterCard are working together to build a model for fraud, well, they probably don't trust each other with the other company's data.

121
00:21:17.390 --> 00:21:27.099
Hart Montgomery: And it might not even be legally possible for them to share data. So we're going to probably have to see some kind of federal-aided learning

122
00:21:27.260 --> 00:21:38.340
Hart Montgomery: protocols become popular, things like that. And of course, there's the whole, post-quantum cryptography and cryptographic agility angle as well.

123
00:21:39.200 --> 00:21:44.390
Hart Montgomery: So, you know, this is kind of a high-level thing.

124
00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:51.010
Hart Montgomery: I'll keep going, because I'm gonna go into some of these in more detail.

125
00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:57.940
Hart Montgomery: But, you know, if you have questions, please feel free to ask. Don't be shy.

126
00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:04.550
Hart Montgomery: One thing we really see as a trend, is code

127
00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:16.570
Hart Montgomery: becoming, sort of, code plus standards. Now, the LF has historically been a code organization, but we've seen a lot of interest in people that want to build standards.

128
00:22:16.830 --> 00:22:20.229
Hart Montgomery: In the recent fu- the recent history.

129
00:22:20.450 --> 00:22:28.650
Hart Montgomery: We see this for a number of reasons. Certainly the trend towards AI is accelerating this.

130
00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:33.219
Hart Montgomery: Ml is bringing down the cost of…

131
00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:41.300
Hart Montgomery: creating new code, but we still need to sort of agree on the interfaces and APIs

132
00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:47.289
Hart Montgomery: If we're all working together. And so, this brings into…

133
00:22:47.460 --> 00:22:51.319
Hart Montgomery: Play, you know, a lot of… a lot more standardization problems.

