Hart Montgomery: If you have any questions about any particular one, I'll do my best to answer it.

146
00:24:41.380 --> 00:24:44.529
Hart Montgomery: And then, you know, there's the question of…

147
00:24:44.650 --> 00:24:54.630
Hart Montgomery: what do we mean by open standards, right? We're open source software at the Linux Foundation, and if we move into open standards, you know, what does this mean? Well…

148
00:24:55.670 --> 00:25:05.650
Hart Montgomery: We want to do standards in a way that are, you know, public specs developed through transparent and open and consensus-driven processes.

149
00:25:05.760 --> 00:25:19.379
Hart Montgomery: This sounds like it might be obvious, but a lot of the existing standards orgs today, are not completely open and transparent. They're not completely, you know.

150
00:25:20.020 --> 00:25:22.549
Hart Montgomery: They're not open, you can't participate.

151
00:25:22.750 --> 00:25:35.160
Hart Montgomery: goes, you know, for everything from ISO, which is kind of the most closed standards org, you know, certainly more modern ones like the ITF and W3C are more open.

152
00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:47.670
Hart Montgomery: But essentially, here, we want to apply our, our code development principles to… Open standards.

153
00:25:48.330 --> 00:25:56.000
Hart Montgomery: I would say a critical thing is that we want our standards orgs to be lightweight.

